\ Spitfire: the flight model, in SI units, of a type whose figures
\ aircraft-types.telic gives. For the Mk I the published figures are the
\ loaded weight, 2,624 kg; the wing, 11.23 m across and 22.48 m²; the
\ Merlin III's 1,030 hp (768 kW), held to its full-throttle height of
\ about 5,500 m and falling with the air's density above it. The lift
\ slope, the drag polar and the propeller's efficiency (0.8 from 120 m/s,
\ less when slower) are estimates chosen so the model reaches the published
\ 582 km/h at 5,669 m and climbs near the published 770 m/min. Each step sums
\ the forces — lift at right angles to the airflow, drag against it, the
\ propeller's thrust along the nose, the weight — and moves the aircraft by
\ them; and sums the moments about the centre of gravity and turns the
\ aircraft by them through its moments of inertia. Each half of the wing
\ meets the air at its own angle, raised on the half going down as the
\ aircraft rolls and on the half the sideslip meets, through the dihedral,
\ and at its own speed as the aircraft yaws; the halves' lift and drag
\ differ, and roll and yaw the aircraft: so the roll is damped, the wing
\ goes down that stalls first, and past the stall a roll feeds itself, the
\ spin. The ailerons change the halves' lift, and drag the aircraft's nose
\ against the roll; the wing's twist weakens them toward the reversal
\ speed. The tailplane restores the angle of attack and damps the pitch,
\ the fin the sideslip and the yaw; the elevator and rudder move them. The
\ pilot moves each control as far as he asked or as far as his strength
\ against the air allows, so the controls stiffen as the speed grows. The
\ gyroscopic coupling of the three rotations is kept; the propeller's
\ torque, slipstream and gyroscopic moments are left out. Past about 15°
\ the wing stalls and lift falls away.
\
\ The fuel is the Spitfire II pilot's notes' 85 gallons, 48 in the upper
\ tank and 37 in the lower, taken as the Mk I's too. The engine burns it in
\ proportion to its power, 86 gallons an hour at full power: an estimate
\ from an assumed 0.6 lb a horsepower-hour at 1,030 hp and 7.2 lb a gallon,
\ not a published figure. With the tanks empty the engine stops. The
\ Hurricane's 97 gallons are its main tanks and a 28-gallon reserve; its
\ fuel cock draws from one or the other, and the engine stops when the
\ tanks it draws from run dry, to run again on the other.
\
\ The flaps are split flaps worked by air, up or down and nothing between
\ (the pilot's notes); they travel in an estimated 2 seconds, adding lift
\ and drag as far as they are down and bringing the nose down, the notes'
\ "strongly nose down" change of trim, which the stick must hold. Each type
\ limits them to its own indicated speed, the Spitfire's notes to 140 mph,
\ the Hurricane's cockpit placard to 120 mph; above an estimated 30 mph
\ more the air blows them back up. The airspeed indicator, like the real one, shows
\ the indicated speed: the true speed scaled by the square root of the
\ air's density over sea level's. The elevator trim's tab, from full
\ nose-down to full nose-up, holds the elevator at up to 0.4 of its travel
\ (an estimate) without the pilot's force, so the stick can rest in the
\ middle at a chosen speed.
\
\ The engine heats its coolant with its power, and the radiator cools it
\ by the air through it, the airspeed and the propeller's wash, against
\ the air's temperature, which falls with height; below 75°C a thermostat
\ (estimated) closes the radiator, shut at 60°C, so the engine stays warm in
\ the cold of 30,000 ft. The coolant settles near
\ 75°C cruising at 1,000 m, reaches 113°C after three minutes' climb at
\ full power, short of the published limit of 120°C, and idling on the
\ ground it passes the limit after about six minutes and boils before ten,
\ as Spitfires overheated taxiing; with the radiator holed it boils within
\ two minutes. The oil's temperature follows the
\ coolant's; its pressure rises with the revolutions and falls as the oil
\ warms. A hit can hole the glycol radiator or the oil system: as the
\ coolant leaks the radiator cools less, and as the oil leaks the pressure
\ falls. Running above 120°C or short of oil pressure wears the engine,
\ and worn out it seizes. Every figure but the 120°C limit is an estimate.
\
\ The throttle sets the boost, from -4 lb closed to the published +6 lb
\ open; the boost cut-out, on 100-octane fuel from 1940, raises it to the
\ published +12 lb for a published five minutes, past which the engine
\ wears. The supercharger holds the boost only up to its ceiling at the
\ height, the rated boost's at the full-throttle height and less above;
\ above it the closed throttle's pressure falls in the same proportion.
\ The pressure at the closed throttle is taken as the engine's friction, so
\ the power is the throttle's opening times the open manifold's pressure
\ over the rated boost's times the revolutions over the rated 3,000: none
\ closed, 1,030 hp open at +6 lb and 1,328 hp at +12 lb, against the
\ published 1,310. The 1939 Spitfire's two-pitch
\ propeller turns at revolutions that follow the boost and the airspeed,
\ fine for taking off and climbing, coarse for speed, and over-revs past
\ 3,000 at speed in fine pitch; the constant-speed propeller fitted from
\ June 1940 holds the revolutions the pilot sets, 1,800 to 3,000 rpm.
\
\ On the ground the aircraft rolls on its wheels: it sits level with the
\ tail free to come up, the stick lifting the nose no more than 12°, until
\ the wing carries its weight. Touching down with the wheels up, too fast
\ downward, or not level, or touching water or a hill, is a crash.

9.81 constant gravity
0.012 constant gear-drag
0.03 constant flap-drag
0.35 constant flap-lift
\ the engine's heat and cooling: the coolant's heating at full power over the cooling's coefficient, °C × m/s; the propeller's wash as airspeed at full revolutions, m/s; the coolant's capacity over the cooling's coefficient, seconds × m/s; the published coolant limit and the estimated temperature at which the glycol boils away, °C
12400 constant engine-heat
30 constant propeller-wash
11160 constant coolant-capacity
120 constant coolant-limit
135 constant coolant-boiling
\ the oil: its temperature following the coolant's over 120 s; its pressure, lb a square inch, at full revolutions when cool, and the pressure below which the bearings wear
120 constant oil-lag
80 constant oil-full-pressure
20 constant oil-low-pressure
\ the elevator trim: the share of the elevator's travel its tab holds at either end of its travel
0.4 constant trim-range
\ the pilot's hand and feet: the share of a control's travel he moves it a second (an estimate)
5 constant control-speed
\ the flaps: their travel, seconds; how far above a type's limit the air blows them back up, mph (an estimate)
2 constant flap-travel-time
30 constant flap-blow-margin

\ the indicated speed, m/s, above which a type's flaps may not be lowered, and the one above which the air blows them back up
: flap-limit-speed ( plane -- speed ) :type @ :flap-limit @ 2.23694 / ;
: flap-blow-speed ( plane -- speed ) :type @ :flap-limit @ flap-blow-margin + 2.23694 / ;
1.6 constant wheel-height
\ the boost, lb a square inch above the standard atmosphere's 14.7: with the throttle closed (an estimate), open, and open with the boost cut-out pushed forward on 100-octane fuel (the published +6 and +12); the emergency boost's published limit, seconds
-4 constant idle-boost
6 constant rated-boost
12 constant emergency-boost
300 constant emergency-limit
14.7 constant atmosphere
\ the propeller's revolutions a minute: the rated 3,000; the two-pitch propeller's at full throttle standing still, fine and coarse, and the airspeed, m/s, that adds as much again (estimates); the constant-speed propeller's lowest setting
3000 constant rated-revolutions
2650 constant fine-revolutions
2100 constant coarse-revolutions
568 constant windmill-speed
1800 constant lowest-revolutions
\ a gallon of petrol's mass, kg: 7.2 lb
3.27 constant fuel-mass
86 constant full-power-flow

variable plane
variable controls
{ :pitch 0 :roll 0 :yaw 0 :brakes false } to controls

\ the aircraft's mass, kg: empty, with the pilot, guns or cameras, and the fuel left
: aircraft-mass ( plane -- kg )
	| plane |
	plane@empty-mass plane@fuel fuel-mass * + ;

\ the air's density at a height, kg/m³
: air-density ( height -- density ) -8500 / exp 1.225 * ;

\ the most absolute pressure the supercharger can hold in the manifold at a height, lb a square inch: the rated boost at the full-throttle height, more below it and less above, with the air's density
: manifold-ceiling ( height rated-height -- pressure )
	| height rated-height |
	atmosphere rated-boost + height air-density rated-height air-density / * ;

\ a new aircraft: where it is, which way it faces, how fast it goes along its nose
: new-plane ( position heading speed on-ground -- plane )
	| position heading speed on-ground |
	heading heading-attitude to attitude
	spitfire-mk-i to type
	{ :type type
	  :position position
	  :velocity attitude@forward speed v*
	  :attitude attitude
	  :rates [ 0 0 0 ]
	  :stick [ 0 0 0 ]
	  :deflections [ 0 0 0 ]
	  :throttle on-ground if 0 else 0.8 then
	  :revolutions on-ground if 0.2 else 0.8 then
	  :propeller :constant-speed
	  :fine true
	  :revolution-setting rated-revolutions
	  :boost-cut-out false
	  :emergency-allowed true
	  :emergency-time 0
	  :boost 0
	  :power 0
	  :flaps false
	  :flap-setting 0
	  :flaps-blown false
	  :trim 0
	  :rudder-trim 0
	  :coolant-temperature on-ground if 60 else 90 then
	  :oil-temperature on-ground if 50 else 70 then
	  :oil-pressure 60
	  :coolant-left 1
	  :oil-left 1
	  :coolant-leak 0
	  :oil-leak 0
	  :engine-wear 0
	  :engine-failure ""
	  :gear on-ground
	  :on-ground on-ground
	  :crashed false
	  :crash-reason ""
	  :angle-of-attack 0
	  :g-load 1
	  :turn-rate 0
	  :fuel type@fuel-capacity
	  :fuel-capacity type@fuel-capacity
	  :reserve-fuel type@reserve-capacity
	  :fuel-cock :main
	  :fuel-starved false
	  :empty-mass type@mass type@fuel-capacity fuel-mass * -
	  :rated-power type@engine-power
	  :full-throttle-height type@full-throttle-height
	  :armed true
	  :ammunition type@fire-seconds
	  :damage 0
	  :engine-dead false
	} ;

\ the lift coefficient at an angle of attack, the flaps adding to it as far as they are down (0 up to 1 down); beyond the stall it falls away
: lift-coefficient ( angle flap-setting type -- coefficient )
	| angle flap-setting type |
	type@stall-angle to stall
	flap-lift flap-setting * to flap-boost
	angle abs stall <= if
		type@lift-slope angle * type@zero-lift-coefficient + flap-boost + exit
	then
	angle 0 > if 1 else -1 then to sign
	type@lift-slope stall * type@zero-lift-coefficient + flap-boost + to peak
	peak angle abs stall - 3 * - 0.5 max2 sign * ;

\ the pilot's hands and feet: the stick and rudder bar moved toward where he asks, at most control-speed of their travel a second; each surface deflected as far as he moves it or as far as his greatest force against the air holds it, [ ailerons elevator rudder ] as shares of their travel
: move-controls ( plane dt -- )
	| plane dt |
	plane@type to type
	plane@position second air-density 0.5 * plane@velocity v-length sq * 1 max2 to pressure
	[ controls@roll controls@pitch controls@yaw ] plane@stick v- to error
	control-speed dt * to reach
	plane@stick [ error first reach negate reach clamp error second reach negate reach clamp error 2 @i reach negate reach clamp ] v+ to stick
	stick plane!stick
	aileron-force type@aileron-stiffness pressure * / 1 min2 to aileron-reach
	elevator-force type@elevator-stiffness pressure * / 1 min2 to elevator-reach
	rudder-force type@rudder-stiffness pressure * / 1 min2 to rudder-reach
	[ stick first aileron-reach negate aileron-reach clamp
	  stick second elevator-reach negate elevator-reach clamp
	  stick 2 @i rudder-reach negate rudder-reach clamp ] plane!deflections ;

\ the forces on the aircraft, ground frame, newtons, and the moments about its centre of gravity, [ roll pitch yaw ] newton-metres rolling right, pitching up and yawing right. Each half of the wing, left then right, meets the air at the angle of attack raised by the roll on the half going down and by the sideslip through the dihedral on the half it meets, at the speed changed by the yaw, its lift less the aileron's on the right as the stick goes right, the ailerons weakened toward the reversal's dynamic pressure; the halves' lift and drag act at the half ellipse's centroid. The pitching moment: the type's offset, the wing's lift about the centre of gravity, the tail's restoring slope, the pitch damping, the elevator with the trim tab's share, the flaps. The yawing moment: the halves' drag, the fin's stability against the sideslip and damping of the yaw, the rudder with the rudder bias's share
: aerodynamics ( plane -- force moments )
	| plane |
	plane@type to type
	plane@velocity to velocity
	velocity v-length to speed
	plane@attitude to attitude
	plane@position second air-density to density
	0.5 density * speed sq * to dynamic-pressure
	attitude velocity ground>attitude spread to ahead to rising to across
	rising negate ahead 0.1 max2 atan2 to angle
	across ahead 0.1 max2 atan2 to slip
	angle plane!angle-of-attack
	plane@power plane@rated-power * to power
	speed 120 / 1 min2 0.01 max2 0.4 ^ type@propeller-efficiency * to efficiency
	power efficiency * speed 1 max2 / type@static-thrust plane@revolutions * min2 to thrust
	attitude@forward thrust v* [ 0 plane aircraft-mass gravity * negate 0 ] v+ to pull-and-weight
	speed 0.5 <= if
		pull-and-weight [ 0 0 0 ] exit
	then
	plane@rates spread to yaw-rate to pitch-rate to roll-rate
	plane@deflections spread to rudder to elevator to aileron
	plane@flap-setting to flaps
	type@wing-area to area
	type@span to span
	type@chord to chord
	type@panel-arm to arm
	1 dynamic-pressure type@reversal-pressure / - to twist
	type@parasite-drag plane@gear if gear-drag + then flap-drag flaps * + to base-drag
	[ 0 0 ] to lifts
	[ 0 0 ] to drags
	[ 0 0 ] to coefficients
	0 2 1 do k
		k 2 * 1 - to side
		angle side roll-rate * arm * speed / + side type@dihedral * slip * + to half-angle
		speed side yaw-rate * arm * - speed / sq to speed-share
		half-angle flaps type lift-coefficient side aileron * type@aileron-lift * twist * - to half-lift
		dynamic-pressure speed-share * area * 0.5 * to half-force
		lifts half-force half-lift * k !i drop
		drags half-force base-drag type@induced-drag half-lift sq * + * k !i drop
		coefficients half-lift k !i drop
	loop
	velocity v-unit to airflow
	attitude@up airflow attitude@up airflow v-dot v* v- v-unit to lift-direction
	lift-direction lifts first lifts second + v* to lift
	airflow drags first drags second + negate v* to drag
	attitude@right across ahead 0.1 max2 / dynamic-pressure * area * -0.6 * v* to sideways
	lifts first lifts second - arm * to roll-moment
	coefficients first coefficients second + 2 / to wing-lift
	type@pitch-offset
	type@wing-arm wing-lift * +
	type@tail-slope angle * -
	type@pitch-damping pitch-rate chord * 2 speed * / * +
	type@elevator-power elevator plane@trim trim-range * + * +
	type@flap-moment flaps * + dynamic-pressure area * chord * * to pitch-moment
	type@yaw-stability slip *
	type@yaw-damping yaw-rate span * 2 speed * / * +
	type@rudder-power rudder plane@rudder-trim trim-range * + * + dynamic-pressure area * span * *
	drags second drags first - arm * + to yaw-moment
	lift drag v+ sideways v+ pull-and-weight v+
	[ roll-moment pitch-moment yaw-moment ] ;

\ the throttle, the propeller and the power: the throttle sets the manifold's pressure between the closed throttle's and the open's, and the boost cut-out raises the open boost to the emergency's; the supercharger holds the open pressure only up to its ceiling at the height, and the closed pressure falls with it above the full-throttle height. The constant-speed propeller's governor holds the revolutions at their setting, idling below a tenth of the throttle; the two-pitch propeller's revolutions follow the boost and the airspeed in fine or coarse pitch. The power, as a share of the rated, is the throttle's opening times the open manifold's pressure over the rated boost's times the revolutions over the rated. Running over the rated revolutions, or on emergency boost past its five minutes, wears the engine. An engine starved of fuel makes no power, as a dead one does, until the cock turns to a tank that has some
: run-propeller ( plane dt -- )
	| plane dt |
	plane@velocity v-length to speed
	plane@position second to height
	plane@boost-cut-out if emergency-boost else rated-boost then to open-boost
	plane@engine-dead plane@fuel-starved or to stopped
	stopped if
		atmosphere height air-density 1.225 / * to manifold
		0 to target
	else
		height plane@full-throttle-height manifold-ceiling to ceiling
		atmosphere open-boost + ceiling min2 to open-manifold
		atmosphere idle-boost + ceiling atmosphere rated-boost + / 1 min2 * to closed-manifold
		open-manifold closed-manifold - plane@throttle * closed-manifold + to manifold
		plane@propeller :constant-speed = if
			plane@throttle 0.1 < if 0.2 else plane@revolution-setting rated-revolutions / then to target
		else
			plane@fine if fine-revolutions else coarse-revolutions then speed windmill-speed / 1 + *
			manifold atmosphere rated-boost + / 0.6 * 0.4 + * rated-revolutions / to target
		then
	then
	manifold atmosphere - plane!boost
	plane@revolutions target over - dt 0.8 * * + plane!revolutions
	stopped if 0 else plane@throttle open-manifold * atmosphere rated-boost + / plane@revolutions * then plane!power
	stopped if exit then
	plane@revolutions 1 > if
		plane@engine-wear plane@revolutions 1 - 0.2 / dt * 60 / + plane!engine-wear
	then
	plane@boost-cut-out plane@throttle 0.9 > and if
		plane@emergency-time dt + plane!emergency-time
		plane@emergency-time emergency-limit > if plane@engine-wear dt 120 / + plane!engine-wear then
	then ;

\ the aircraft made a type, its figures aircraft-types.telic's: its engine, its tanks full, its mass, its guns' seconds of fire
: use-type ( plane type -- )
	| plane type |
	type plane!type
	type@engine-power plane!rated-power
	type@full-throttle-height plane!full-throttle-height
	type@fuel-capacity plane!fuel-capacity
	type@fuel-capacity plane!fuel
	type@reserve-capacity plane!reserve-fuel
	type@mass type@fuel-capacity fuel-mass * - plane!empty-mass
	type@fire-seconds plane!ammunition ;

\ the aircraft made the photographic reconnaissance PR IV, unarmed
: make-reconnaissance ( plane -- )
	| plane |
	plane spitfire-pr-iv use-type
	false plane!armed
	0 plane!ammunition ;

\ the speed the airspeed indicator shows: the true speed scaled by the square root of the air's density over sea level's, m/s
: indicated-speed ( plane -- speed )
	| plane |
	plane@velocity v-length plane@position second air-density 1.225 / sqrt * ;

\ the flaps moving toward the selector's position over their travel time; blown back up when the indicated speed passes the blow-back speed
: move-flaps ( plane dt -- )
	| plane dt |
	plane@flaps plane indicated-speed plane flap-blow-speed > and if
		false plane!flaps
		true plane!flaps-blown
	then
	plane@flaps if 1 else 0 then to target
	dt flap-travel-time / to step
	plane@flap-setting target - abs step <= if
		target plane!flap-setting
	else
		plane@flap-setting target plane@flap-setting > if step else step negate then + plane!flap-setting
	then ;

\ the air's temperature at a height, °C: 15 at sea level, falling 6.5 a kilometre
: air-temperature ( height -- celsius ) 0.0065 * 15 swap - ;

\ the engine's step: the coolant heated by the power and cooled by the air through the radiator, less as the glycol leaks away; the oil's temperature following it, the oil's pressure with the revolutions, less as the oil warms and leaks; the wear of running hot or short of oil, the engine seizing when it is worn out
: run-engine ( plane dt -- )
	| plane dt |
	plane@power to power
	plane@position second air-temperature to outside
	plane@velocity v-length plane@revolutions propeller-wash * + to airflow
	plane@coolant-left plane@coolant-leak dt * - 0 max2 plane!coolant-left
	plane@oil-left plane@oil-leak dt * - 0 max2 plane!oil-left
	plane@coolant-left to coolant
	plane@coolant-temperature to temperature
	temperature 60 - 15 / 0 1 clamp to thermostat
	engine-heat power * airflow coolant * thermostat * temperature outside - * - coolant-capacity coolant 0.1 max2 * / dt * temperature + to temperature
	temperature plane!coolant-temperature
	plane@oil-temperature temperature 0.75 * plane@oil-temperature - dt * oil-lag / + to oil
	oil plane!oil-temperature
	plane@engine-dead if 0 else
		oil-full-pressure plane@revolutions sqrt * oil 70 - 0.3 * - 0 max2 plane@oil-left 3 * 1 min2 *
	then to pressure
	pressure plane!oil-pressure
	plane@engine-dead if exit then
	temperature coolant-limit > if
		plane@engine-wear temperature coolant-limit - coolant-boiling coolant-limit - / dt * 60 / + plane!engine-wear
	then
	temperature coolant-boiling > if plane@engine-wear dt 10 / + plane!engine-wear then
	pressure oil-low-pressure < plane@revolutions 0.1 > and if
		plane@engine-wear oil-low-pressure pressure - oil-low-pressure / dt * 30 / + plane!engine-wear
	then
	plane@engine-wear 1 >= if
		true plane!engine-dead
		temperature coolant-limit > if "THE GLYCOL HAS BOILED AND THE ENGINE HAS SEIZED" else "THE OIL PRESSURE HAS GONE AND THE ENGINE HAS SEIZED" then plane!engine-failure
	then ;

\ the aircraft's rotation for a step: the moments, less the gyroscopic coupling of its three rotations, turn it through its moments of inertia, the fuel's share of them as the tanks hold it; worked in the right-handed axes along the span, the vertical and the line of flight, where pitching up, rolling right and yawing right are a negative, a negative and a positive turn. On the ground the wheels hold the wings level, the tail wheel's drag stops the yaw within a third of a second (an estimate), and the nose rises no more than 12° nor goes below level. The attitude then turns by the rates
: rotate-aircraft ( plane moments dt -- )
	| plane moments dt |
	plane@type to type
	plane@fuel plane@fuel-capacity / to fuel-share
	type@dry-inertia type@fuel-inertia fuel-share v* v+ spread to yaw-inertia to pitch-inertia to roll-inertia
	plane@rates spread to yaw-rate to pitch-rate to roll-rate
	moments spread to yaw-moment to pitch-moment to roll-moment
	pitch-rate negate to across-spin
	yaw-rate to vertical-spin
	roll-rate negate to along-spin
	pitch-moment negate roll-inertia yaw-inertia - vertical-spin * along-spin * - pitch-inertia / dt * across-spin + to across-spin
	yaw-moment pitch-inertia roll-inertia - along-spin * across-spin * - yaw-inertia / dt * vertical-spin + to vertical-spin
	roll-moment negate yaw-inertia pitch-inertia - across-spin * vertical-spin * - roll-inertia / dt * along-spin + to along-spin
	across-spin negate to pitch-rate
	vertical-spin to yaw-rate
	along-spin negate to roll-rate
	plane@attitude to attitude
	plane@on-ground if
		0 to roll-rate
		yaw-rate 1 dt 3 * - 0 max2 * to yaw-rate
		attitude@forward second asin to nose-angle
		nose-angle 0.21 >= pitch-rate 0 > and if 0 to pitch-rate then
		nose-angle 0 <= pitch-rate 0 < and if 0 to pitch-rate then
	then
	[ roll-rate pitch-rate yaw-rate ] plane!rates
	attitude :right pitch-rate dt * negate turn-attitude
	:forward roll-rate dt * negate turn-attitude
	:up yaw-rate dt * turn-attitude
	square-attitude to attitude
	plane attitude :attitude ! drop ;

\ the aircraft touching the ground: a landing if level, slow downward and on its wheels over land, a crash otherwise; the land and water judged by the water's region nearest the aircraft, not the one last drawn
: touch-ground ( plane -- )
	| plane |
	plane@position to position
	position first position 2 @i ground-height to ground
	position second ground wheel-height + > if plane false :on-ground ! drop exit then
	plane@velocity second to sinking
	plane@attitude@up second to level
	position use-water-near
	position first position 2 @i water-at? if plane true :crashed ! "ditched in the sea" :crash-reason ! drop exit then
	ground 0 > if plane true :crashed ! "flew into the hillside" :crash-reason ! drop exit then
	plane@gear not if plane true :crashed ! "landed with the wheels up" :crash-reason ! drop exit then
	sinking -4 < level 0.93 < or if plane true :crashed ! "struck the ground" :crash-reason ! drop exit then
	plane true :on-ground ! [ position first ground wheel-height + position 2 @i ] :position ! drop
	plane@velocity to velocity
	[ velocity first 0 velocity 2 @i ] to rolling
	rolling v-length to rolling-speed
	controls@brakes if 0.45 else 0.04 then gravity * to deceleration
	rolling-speed 0.05 > if
		rolling rolling-speed deceleration plane :dt @ * - 0 max2 rolling-speed / v* to rolling
	then
	plane rolling :velocity ! drop
	plane@attitude to attitude
	attitude@forward to forward
	forward second 0 < if
		[ forward first 0 forward 2 @i ] v-unit to level-forward
		plane { :forward level-forward :up attitude@up :right attitude@right } square-attitude :attitude ! drop
	then ;

\ a step's fuel, burned in proportion to the power from the tanks the cock selects: the main tanks, or the reserve where the type has one; the engine starved while the selected tanks are dry
: burn-fuel ( plane dt -- )
	| plane dt |
	plane@power full-power-flow * 3600 / dt * to burn
	plane@reserve-fuel to reserve
	plane@fuel reserve - to main
	plane@fuel-cock :reserve = if
		reserve burn - 0 max2 to reserve
		reserve plane!reserve-fuel
	else
		main burn - 0 max2 to main
	then
	main reserve + plane!fuel
	plane@fuel-cock :reserve = if reserve else main then 0 <= plane!fuel-starved ;

\ one step of flight, dt seconds
: fly ( plane dt -- )
	| plane dt |
	plane@crashed if exit then
	plane dt :dt ! drop
	plane dt burn-fuel
	plane@engine-dead if 0 plane!throttle then
	plane dt run-propeller
	plane dt move-controls
	plane aerodynamics to moments to force
	force plane aircraft-mass 1 swap / v* to acceleration
	plane@velocity acceleration dt v* v+ to velocity
	plane velocity :velocity ! drop
	plane plane@position velocity dt v* v+ :position ! drop
	acceleration [ 0 gravity 0 ] v+ plane@attitude@up v-dot gravity / plane!g-load
	plane@attitude@forward heading-of to old-heading
	plane dt move-flaps
	plane dt run-engine
	plane moments dt rotate-aircraft
	plane@attitude@forward heading-of old-heading - 540 + 360 mod 180 - dt / plane!turn-rate
	plane touch-ground ;
